Leave a seekable standard input after the last byte head used.
POSIX requires a utility that stops before end-of-file on seekable
input to leave the offset just past the last byte processed. head reads
standard input through stdio, which reads ahead in blocks. glibc and
musl move the descriptor back to the stream position when they close
stdin at exit, but the Windows C runtime does not, so after "head -n 2"
the shared file offset was at the end of the block rather than after
line 2, and "(head -n 2; cat) <file" lost the lines in between.

On Windows, before exiting, if standard input is not at EOF, seek its
descriptor to the stream position. For a pipe the seek simply fails.
Other systems are left alone, since there the C library's adjustment
at exit would move the offset a second time.

---
 coreutils/head.c |   11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/head.c
+++ b/coreutils/head.c
@@ -268,5 +268,16 @@
 		fmt = header_fmt_str;
 	} while (*++argv);
 
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+	/* POSIX: leave a seekable stdin just past the last byte used.
+	 * The C library does this when it closes stdin at exit, but
+	 * the Windows C runtime doesn't. */
+	if (!feof(stdin)) {
+		off_t pos = ftello(stdin);
+		if (pos >= 0)
+			lseek(STDIN_FILENO, pos, SEEK_SET);
+	}
+#endif
+
 	fflush_stdout_and_exit(retval);
 }
